To generate the appropriate assert statement for the given comment, we need to ensure that after the constructor call, the `CircularFifoQueue` instance contains exactly the same elements as the input collection `coll`. Since the `addAll` method is used to populate the queue, we can assume that the queue will have the same elements as the collection.

Here's the assert statement that captures this specification:

<code>assert this.size() == coll.size() && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < coll.size(); this.contains(coll.toArray()[(i + this.start) % this.maxElements]);</code>

However, the above assertion is quite complex due to the circular nature of the queue. A simpler and more direct way to assert equality between the queue and the collection would be to check if the queue contains all elements of the collection and vice versa. Given the constraints and the fact that the queue cannot contain null elements, we can simplify it as follows:

<code>assert this.size() == coll.size() && coll.containsAll(this) && this.containsAll(coll);</code>

This ensures that both the queue and the collection have the same size and contain the same elements.

Thus, the final assert statement is:

<code>assert this.size() == coll.size() && coll.containsAll(this) && this.containsAll(coll);</code>
